<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Generalized tonic-clonic seizure on a flight two and a half weeks ago in a 36-year-old former Olympic sprinter who still trains daily, with treatment for seizures elsewhere in the past (records requested) and no antiseizure medication now. EEG and MRI of the brain with susceptibility-weighted imaging are booked this week. Please see her within two weeks to interpret them, decide whether this is epilepsy, and advise on treatment and safety. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is this epilepsy, and what do the EEG and MRI show about its cause?</item><item>Which antiseizure medication do you recommend for a woman who could become pregnant, and what counseling should go with it?</item><item>If the MRI shows a structural lesion, such as a vascular malformation, scar or tumor, should she see neurosurgery about removing it?</item><item>What should she be told about SUDEP, nocturnal monitoring, and safety with water, heights and sleep?</item><item>When can she return to sprint training, flying and driving?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Seizure on a flight two and a half weeks ago, with stiffening and then jerking of all four limbs described by others and about half an hour of confusion afterwards; observed overnight in hospital elsewhere and discharged the next day. She remembers nothing of the seizure itself. Treated for seizures elsewhere in the past; records requested. No antiseizure medication now. No head injury, fever, alcohol excess, drug use or sleep loss before the seizure. Examination today normal. Results today: blood count, electrolytes, glucose, calcium, magnesium, kidney and liver tests and creatine kinase normal; pregnancy test negative. Not planning a pregnancy. Not driving from today; the report California requires of physicians has been explained to her and will be sent. She wants the details kept private [S]</paragraph><list><item>A seizure on a flight two and a half weeks ago, after which she was kept in hospital overnight; she remembers nothing of the seizure itself and wants to understand what happened. [S]</item><item>She wants to know why it happened and how to stop it happening again, and whether she can keep training, driving and flying. [S]</item><item>She wants the details kept private. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Florence Griffith was born in Los Angeles on December 21, 1959, the seventh of eleven children, and grew up from the age of four in a public housing project in the Watts neighborhood of Los Angeles. Reference works give her given names in both orders, Delorez Florence and Florence Delorez. [D]</item><item>She ran for California State University, Northridge, and then for UCLA, graduating in 1983, and won the silver medal in the 200 meters at the 1984 Olympic Games in Los Angeles, competing as Florence Griffith. [D]</item><item>In 1988 she set a world record of 10.49 seconds for 100 meters at the U.S. Olympic Trials and, at the Seoul Olympic Games, won gold medals in the 100 meters, the 200 meters (in a world record of 21.34 seconds) and the 4 x 100 meters relay, and a silver medal in the 4 x 400 meters relay. She retired from competition in 1989. [D]</item><item>In 1993 she was appointed co-chair of the President&apos;s Council on Physical Fitness and Sports, and in 1995 she was inducted into the U.S. Track and Field Hall of Fame. She also worked as a designer of clothing and athletic wear. [D]</item><item>In April 1996 she had a seizure on an airplane on the way to St. Louis. She was taken to hospital and discharged the next day, and her family asked that no other details be released. [D]</item><item>Coroner&apos;s investigation records, reported after her death, state that she had a generalized (grand mal) seizure in 1990 and had been treated for seizures in 1990, 1993 and 1994. [D]</item></list><paragraph>1 later historical claim is omitted because it describes events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

